                                99 Bottles Of Beer: A Play in Four Scenes.


Benedick, a man who learns to store numbers.
Beatrice, a woman who loses her mind as she speaks to members of the court.
Arthur, a king from A.
Benvolio, a nobleman from B.
Dogberry, an officer from D.
Escalus, a prince from E.
Fortinbras, a prince from F.
Hamlet, a prince from H.
Imogen, a noblewoman from I.
Hecate, a fairy queen from K.
Leonato, a nobleman from L.
Duncan, a king from N.
Orlando, a prince from O.
Prospero, a sorcerer from P.
Rosalind, a noblewoman from R.
Sebastian, a representative from S.
Shallow, a justice of the peace who moves to S.
Tybalt, a nobleman from T.
Claudio, a soldier from U.
Falstaff, a foolish nobleman from W.
Juliet, a noblewoman from an empty space.
Cressida, a woman who pauses while speaking.
Puck, a fairy from another time period.
Oberon, a fairy king full of excitement.
Romeo, a nobleman in search of a new line.



                        Act I: In which we count to one hundred.

    Scene I: Beatrice meets the nobility so that she might recall them later on.

[Enter Beatrice and Arthur]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of a lovely sweet warm fine healthy blossoming rose and a king.
[Exit Arthur]

[Enter Benvolio]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of Arthur and a king.
[Exit Benvolio]

[Enter Dogberry]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of Benvolio and twice an angel.
[Exit Dogberry]

[Enter Escalus]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of Arthur and an honest noble king.
[Exit Escalus]

[Enter Fortinbras]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of Escalus and a flower.
[Exit Fortinbras]

[Enter Hamlet]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of Fortinbras and twice a king.
[Exit Hamlet]

[Enter Imogen]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of Escalus and your fine golden hair.
[Exit Imogen]

[Enter Hecate]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of Imogen and a yellow flower.
[Exit Hecate]

[Enter Leonato]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of Benvolio and twice the sum of a fine red rose and a king.
[Exit Leonato]

[Enter Duncan]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of Leonato and an honest king.
[Exit Duncan]

[Enter Orlando]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of Duncan and a summer's day.
[Exit Orlando]

[Enter Prospero]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of Orlando and an angel.
[Exit Prospero]

[Enter Rosalind]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of Prospero and a beautiful cat.
[Exit Rosalind]

[Enter Sebastian]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of Rosalind and a king.
[Exit Sebastian]

[Enter Tybalt]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of Rosalind and a proud pony.
[Exit Tybalt]

[Enter Claudio]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of Escalus and a beautiful lovely sweet warm summer's day.
[Exit Claudio]

[Enter Falstaff]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of Claudio and twice a king.
[Exit Falstaff]

[Enter Juliet]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of a beautiful fine sweet lovely angel and a beautiful fine fair sunny summer's day.
[Exit Juliet]

[Enter Cressida]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of the sum of Juliet and an honest noble rich king and a warm sunny summer's day.
[Exit Cressida]

[Enter Puck]
Beatrice: Thou art the difference between Cressida and a fat toad.
[Exit Puck]

[Enter Oberon]
Beatrice: Thou art the sum of Juliet and a flower.
[Exit Oberon]

[Enter Romeo]
Beatrice: Thou art the difference between Sebastian and Imogen.
[Exit Romeo]


                           Scene II: Beatrice and Benedick meet for the first time.

[Enter Benedick]
Beatrice: Thou art a tree.
Benedick: Thou art a healthy beautiful fine fair sunny warm golden summer's day.
    Thou art the sum of thyself and a vile disgusting dirty cowardly goat.
    Thou art the sum of thyself and a miserable fatherless half-witted hound.
    Thou art the sum of thyself and a foul fat-kidneyed pig.

                                Scene III: Beatrice meets everyone again.

Beatrice: Open thy heart!
[Exit Benedick]

[Enter Juliet]
Beatrice: Speak thy mind!
[Exit Juliet]

[Enter Benvolio]
Beatrice: Speak thy mind!
